WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE Module 4: WRDS MCP, Scale, and the Stata Bridge narrated walkthrough. Generated from the
narration script and per-segment audio durations (0.7 s gap per slide).

s01-001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.009
Welcome back.

s01-002
00:00:01.059 --> 00:00:06.884
This is Module 4 of Claude Code for Accounting
Research: WRDS MCP, scale discipline,

s01-003
00:00:06.934 --> 00:00:09.431
and the Stata bridge. I'm John Barrios.

s01-004
00:00:09.481 --> 00:00:14.684
Today you connect Claude Code to WRDS
on your own account and pull real

s01-005
00:00:14.734 --> 00:00:18.670
Compustat data into a panel
you can run a regression on.

s02-006
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.540
The session runs in five blocks.

s02-007
00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:22.894
Forty minutes of lecture.

s02-008
00:00:22.944 --> 00:00:24.668
A thirty-five minute demo,

s02-009
00:00:24.718 --> 00:00:28.806
where I go from a plain-English request
to a regression-ready funda extract.

s02-010
00:00:28.856 --> 00:00:30.104
A ten-minute break.

s02-011
00:00:30.154 --> 00:00:35.207
An eighty-minute lab on two tracks,
live WRDS or a public fallback.

s02-012
00:00:35.257 --> 00:00:37.370
Then the debrief and the exit ticket.

s03-013
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:44.102
So Module 3 ended with the filing text
parsed and each firm identified by its CIK.

s03-014
00:00:44.152 --> 00:00:48.033
The next step is to attach fundamentals:
leverage, profitability, size.

s03-015
00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:49.278
Those live in Compustat and CRSP,

s03-016
00:00:49.328 --> 00:00:56.274
and getting to them means dealing with
authentication, licensing, and scale.

s03-017
00:00:56.324 --> 00:01:00.145
Authentication, because WRDS is a licensed
account behind multi-factor login.

s03-018
00:01:00.195 --> 00:01:05.161
Licensing, because the subscription
belongs to your institution,

s03-019
00:01:05.211 --> 00:01:07.950
and what you pull isn't
yours to redistribute.

s03-020
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.110
And scale, because the annual file has
hundreds of variables and decades of history.

s03-021
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.559
A chat tool can't handle any of them.

s03-022
00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:19.030
Today we set up each one,
in that order, on a real account.

s04-023
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.199
MCP stands for Model Context Protocol.

s04-024
00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:27.209
Instead of you writing the plumbing
between the agent and an outside service,

s04-025
00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:30.433
meaning the connection, the login,
and the query submission,

s04-026
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:33.131
a small dedicated server does that job.

s04-027
00:01:33.181 --> 00:01:36.271
The agent talks to the server
through a standard protocol.

s04-028
00:01:36.321 --> 00:01:40.267
wrds-mcp is that server for WRDS.

s04-029
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:44.897
It's a Python process running on your machine,
and it exposes sixteen named tools,

s04-030
00:01:44.947 --> 00:01:48.864
like run SQL, download data,
and helpers for CRSP, Compustat,

s04-031
00:01:48.914 --> 00:01:50.172
and the CRSP-Compustat merge.

s04-032
00:01:50.222 --> 00:01:52.612
It reads your credentials
from the environment,

s04-033
00:01:52.662 --> 00:01:58.478
so your password never appears in
a script or in the conversation.

s04-034
00:01:58.528 --> 00:02:01.090
This is rung five of the
ladder from Module 1.

s05-035
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.016
Here's the chain, from left to right.

s05-036
00:02:04.066 --> 00:02:07.760
You type a plain request in
Claude Code, with no password in it.

s05-037
00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.857
That goes to the wrds-mcp server,

s05-038
00:02:10.907 --> 00:02:15.712
a local process that reads your username
and password from its configuration on

s05-039
00:02:15.762 --> 00:02:16.717
your disk.

s05-040
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:22.206
The tunnel uses paramiko to open one SSH
session to WRDS, takes a single Duo push,

s05-041
00:02:22.256 --> 00:02:25.289
and stays open on local port 49600.

s05-042
00:02:25.339 --> 00:02:28.201
It reads the same credentials
from the env file.

s05-043
00:02:28.251 --> 00:02:31.844
Then WRDS PostgreSQL itself,
with schemas like comp and crsp.

s05-044
00:02:31.894 --> 00:02:36.329
Downloads land in a folder called
WRDS data in your home directory,

s05-045
00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:40.267
and Claude sees a summary,
like a row count.

s05-046
00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:44.674
People often assume Claude must have
your password to pull WRDS data,

s05-047
00:02:44.724 --> 00:02:48.110
but the password stays in those
files and never reaches Claude.

s06-048
00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.135
This distinction applies well beyond WRDS.

s06-049
00:02:52.185 --> 00:02:57.619
A sandbox, like a container or a locked-down
shell, limits what an agent can do.

s06-050
00:02:57.669 --> 00:03:01.994
It does nothing to limit what the agent
can see once data is in front of it.

s06-051
00:03:02.044 --> 00:03:04.609
What protects your password is
where it sits: in an env file,

s06-052
00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:08.462
one link below where
Claude does its reasoning.

s06-053
00:03:08.512 --> 00:03:10.944
You're not relying on
Claude's good behavior.

s06-054
00:03:10.994 --> 00:03:14.383
Data is a separate matter,
and it's a legal one.

s06-055
00:03:14.433 --> 00:03:17.461
The WRDS license belongs
to your institution,

s06-056
00:03:17.511 --> 00:03:19.852
and extracts aren't yours to redistribute.

s06-057
00:03:19.902 --> 00:03:23.642
So the course rule is zero
WRDS data distribution.

s06-058
00:03:23.692 --> 00:03:27.850
That means no extracts in a lab zip,
on the course site, or in Slack.

s07-059
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:32.073
Now the routine habits,
done in order every session.

s07-060
00:03:32.123 --> 00:03:37.976
Install wrds-mcp in its own virtual
environment, not your system Python.

s07-061
00:03:38.026 --> 00:03:43.309
Put your credentials in the ~/.wrds-mcp.env
file in your home directory,

s07-062
00:03:43.359 --> 00:03:45.165
and chmod it to 600 so
only you can read it.

s07-063
00:03:45.215 --> 00:03:51.556
If you're in a git project, write the
gitignore entry before that file exists.

s07-064
00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:53.174
Then the tunnel: up, work, teardown.

s07-065
00:03:53.224 --> 00:03:58.387
Run tunnel up, approve the one Duo push,
and confirm port 49600 is listening.

s07-066
00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:01.918
One approved session covers
every query in the run.

s07-067
00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:05.310
Then run tunnel down at
the end of every session.

s07-068
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.710
People skip that step most often,
because nothing visibly breaks when they do.

s08-069
00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.733
The hardest mistake to undo is pasting
your WRDS password into a prompt,

s08-070
00:04:15.783 --> 00:04:16.634
just this once.

s08-071
00:04:16.684 --> 00:04:19.696
A lot of times it happens
because a script errored,

s08-072
00:04:19.746 --> 00:04:24.652
and pasting the password felt faster
than fixing the environment variable.

s08-073
00:04:24.702 --> 00:04:28.942
Once a credential is typed into the
conversation, it has been sent to the model,

s08-074
00:04:28.992 --> 00:04:31.335
and no cleanup afterward
fully undoes that.

s08-075
00:04:31.385 --> 00:04:33.444
So the fix is procedural.

s08-076
00:04:33.494 --> 00:04:35.498
Credentials go in the env file.

s08-077
00:04:35.548 --> 00:04:38.728
If a script can't find them,
check that the env file is sourced,

s08-078
00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.250
and don't hardcode a value to move faster.

s09-079
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.159
These are the four Compustat filters,
and every pull from funda uses all four.

s09-080
00:04:47.209 --> 00:04:49.888
indfmt = 'INDL', for industrial format.

s09-081
00:04:49.938 --> 00:04:51.591
datafmt = 'STD', for standardized data.

s09-082
00:04:51.641 --> 00:04:55.471
popsrc = 'D', for the domestic population.

s09-083
00:04:55.521 --> 00:04:58.804
And consol = 'C',
for consolidated statements.

s09-084
00:04:58.854 --> 00:05:02.480
If you leave one out, the query doesn't
error, and the rows look reasonable.

s09-085
00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:06.505
Drop the industrial-format filter,

s09-086
00:05:06.555 --> 00:05:10.311
and financial-services records
load next to the industrial ones.

s09-087
00:05:10.361 --> 00:05:12.109
Drop the consolidation filter,

s09-088
00:05:12.159 --> 00:05:16.892
and unconsolidated records load
beside the consolidated statements,

s09-089
00:05:16.942 --> 00:05:20.177
so the same business gets counted twice.

s09-090
00:05:20.227 --> 00:05:23.041
That shows up later as wrong coefficients.

s09-091
00:05:23.091 --> 00:05:27.632
The WRDS filter skill in the
course catalog enforces all four.

s09-092
00:05:27.682 --> 00:05:30.190
It's built the same way as
the skills from Module 3.

s10-093
00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:35.650
CRSP is different, because there's
no iron-law list to fall back on.

s10-094
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:39.749
The CRSP returns helper joins the returns
file to the names file by permno and

s10-095
00:05:39.799 --> 00:05:42.808
date range, and that's all it does.

s10-096
00:05:42.858 --> 00:05:47.070
Share-type and exchange restrictions
aren't applied by any tool in this stack.

s10-097
00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.502
You apply them yourself,
after the pull, every time.

s10-098
00:05:49.552 --> 00:05:52.431
So the order is the
same for both databases.

s10-099
00:05:52.481 --> 00:05:53.547
Figure out which filters apply,

s10-100
00:05:53.597 --> 00:05:58.635
check that the query includes them before
you run it, run it, and then inspect.

s10-101
00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:01.551
Do that for a quick test too.

s10-102
00:06:01.601 --> 00:06:04.325
If a quick test skips a filter
and the output looks fine,

s10-103
00:06:04.375 --> 00:06:06.570
you still haven't validated anything.

s11-104
00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:11.216
After every pull, and before you
tell anyone the extract is good,

s11-105
00:06:11.266 --> 00:06:14.253
including yourself, you run four checks.

s11-106
00:06:14.303 --> 00:06:17.700
The row count, compared with a
number you expected going in.

s11-107
00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:22.079
A look at actual values with head or
sample, not just the column names.

s11-108
00:06:22.129 --> 00:06:24.656
A null check on the
columns you plan to use.

s11-109
00:06:24.706 --> 00:06:26.310
And a date-range check,

s11-110
00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.266
the min and max of fyear against
what the query should have returned.

s11-111
00:06:30.316 --> 00:06:32.162
All four together take under a minute,

s11-112
00:06:32.212 --> 00:06:35.107
and you run them even when
the query finished cleanly.

s11-113
00:06:35.157 --> 00:06:37.990
No error only tells you the SQL was valid.

s12-114
00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:40.953
This is the Rationalization Table:

s12-115
00:06:41.003 --> 00:06:45.056
the things people tell themselves right
before they report a number they never

s12-116
00:06:45.106 --> 00:06:45.511
checked.

s12-117
00:06:45.561 --> 00:06:46.962
It ran without an error.

s12-118
00:06:47.012 --> 00:06:48.494
The row count looks about right,

s12-119
00:06:48.544 --> 00:06:52.702
which isn't the same as comparing it
with a number you wrote down beforehand.

s12-120
00:06:52.752 --> 00:06:56.878
I've pulled this table before,
which says nothing about this query,

s12-121
00:06:56.928 --> 00:06:58.972
on this date range, with these filters.

s12-122
00:06:59.022 --> 00:07:03.400
The last row does the most damage:
the query worked, so it's correct.

s12-123
00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:07.678
Whether the query executed and whether the
data is correct are separate questions,

s12-124
00:07:07.728 --> 00:07:10.690
and only the inspection
checks answer the second one.

s13-125
00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.182
On scale, filter at the
database, not in pandas.

s13-126
00:07:15.232 --> 00:07:18.019
The WHERE clause restricts
by year, by the iron laws,

s13-127
00:07:18.069 --> 00:07:23.818
by a list of G V keys or an SIC range,
before a single row crosses the wire.

s13-128
00:07:23.868 --> 00:07:25.305
And never SELECT * on a WRDS table.

s13-129
00:07:25.355 --> 00:07:26.913
Name the columns you need.

s13-130
00:07:26.963 --> 00:07:32.000
Compustat's annual file has hundreds
of variables and decades of history for

s13-131
00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:33.772
tens of thousands of firms,

s13-132
00:07:33.822 --> 00:07:38.486
so you never read it whole into
a chat window or a data frame.

s13-133
00:07:38.536 --> 00:07:41.714
Module 1 warned you about large
CSVs in the context window,

s13-134
00:07:41.764 --> 00:07:45.390
and the same mechanics apply
here at a much higher cost.

s14-135
00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:50.287
Once a filtered, narrow extract
is on disk, convert it to Parquet,

s14-136
00:07:50.337 --> 00:07:52.779
which is compressed and stored by column.

s14-137
00:07:52.829 --> 00:07:54.484
In the course's own test,

s14-138
00:07:54.534 --> 00:07:59.507
a filtered Compustat CSV extract shrank
to roughly one-fifteenth of its size as

s14-139
00:07:59.557 --> 00:08:00.504
Parquet.

s14-140
00:08:00.554 --> 00:08:04.585
That number comes from our own conversion,
and you can run a test like it yourself.

s14-141
00:08:04.635 --> 00:08:07.329
Then query the file with DuckDB,

s14-142
00:08:07.379 --> 00:08:10.283
which reads only the columns
and row groups a query touches.

s14-143
00:08:10.333 --> 00:08:15.850
At the end, export to Stata only the
analysis-ready table, as a .dta file.

s15-144
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:19.700
Compustat's variable names
don't explain themselves.

s15-145
00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:22.017
dltt, che, csho, prcc_f.

s15-146
00:08:22.067 --> 00:08:25.054
A fresh session, or a coauthor,

s15-147
00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:29.857
can't know that dltt is long-term debt
in millions of dollars unless something

s15-148
00:08:29.907 --> 00:08:31.589
on disk says so.

s15-149
00:08:31.639 --> 00:08:37.773
So you build a metadata table once,
one row per variable: the name, a plain label,

s15-150
00:08:37.823 --> 00:08:39.536
the units, and a screening note.

s15-151
00:08:39.586 --> 00:08:41.711
In the demo I close the
session, open a new one,

s15-152
00:08:41.761 --> 00:08:46.545
and orient it with nothing
but SELECT * from metadata.

s15-153
00:08:46.595 --> 00:08:52.540
It's Module 1's first mantra, files persist
and context doesn't, used for your data.

s15-154
00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:58.055
The table also survives handing the
project to a coauthor who never saw your

s15-155
00:08:58.105 --> 00:08:58.630
prompts.

s16-156
00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:03.151
Before you build anything
nontrivial, ask what's involved.

s16-157
00:09:03.201 --> 00:09:06.825
Use this wording as written,
not a paraphrase.

s16-158
00:09:06.875 --> 00:09:11.309
I want a firm-year panel with leverage
and ROA for US industrials 2000 to 2023

s16-159
00:09:11.359 --> 00:09:12.033
from funda.

s16-160
00:09:12.083 --> 00:09:16.532
Tell me what's involved before
you try anything complicated.

s16-161
00:09:16.582 --> 00:09:18.352
What might be missing?

s16-162
00:09:18.402 --> 00:09:21.907
The prompt asks Claude to reason
about the task before any SQL runs:

s16-163
00:09:21.957 --> 00:09:25.828
which filters apply,
which variables it needs,

s16-164
00:09:25.878 --> 00:09:29.117
where a link to CRSP could create
duplicate rows. In the demo,

s16-165
00:09:29.167 --> 00:09:34.149
the filter question and the linking
caveat both come up before a single row is

s16-166
00:09:34.199 --> 00:09:34.610
pulled.

s17-167
00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:39.705
Here's what that looks like, as a
stylized transcript, not a screenshot.

s17-168
00:09:39.755 --> 00:09:45.535
The request is plain English: annual
Compustat fundamentals, 2000 to 2023,

s17-169
00:09:45.585 --> 00:09:49.074
with gvkey, fyear, sales,
and total assets, saved as Parquet.

s17-170
00:09:49.124 --> 00:09:53.329
Underneath is the call to the
download tool and the SQL it runs.

s17-171
00:09:53.379 --> 00:09:58.516
It has an explicit column list, all four
iron-law filters, and the year range.

s17-172
00:09:58.566 --> 00:09:59.702
Look at the max rows setting.

s17-173
00:09:59.752 --> 00:10:05.757
By default the download tool stops
at 100,000 rows without telling you,

s17-174
00:10:05.807 --> 00:10:08.216
so set it above the count you expect.

s17-175
00:10:08.266 --> 00:10:12.577
The file lands in your WRDS data
folder, not in the conversation.

s17-176
00:10:12.627 --> 00:10:15.870
There are no credentials and
no data values anywhere in it.

s18-177
00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.151
Module 2 said we'd come
back to sub-agents,

s18-178
00:10:19.201 --> 00:10:23.106
and they belong here because
scale is the problem they solve.

s18-179
00:10:23.156 --> 00:10:27.375
A sub-agent is a separate Claude instance
with its own fresh context and one

s18-180
00:10:27.425 --> 00:10:28.654
bounded job.

s18-181
00:10:28.704 --> 00:10:31.053
Its intermediate output stays inside it,

s18-182
00:10:31.103 --> 00:10:34.371
and only a written summary
comes back to your main session.

s18-183
00:10:34.421 --> 00:10:39.533
Independent pieces can run in parallel,
one per decade, industry, or filing batch.

s18-184
00:10:39.583 --> 00:10:44.010
You can define your own as Markdown files
in the agents folder inside .claude,

s18-185
00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:45.925
and ask Claude to create or edit them.

s18-186
00:10:45.975 --> 00:10:47.305
In the slide's example,

s18-187
00:10:47.355 --> 00:10:52.116
three sub-agents each profile one
decade of the funda Parquet file,

s18-188
00:10:52.166 --> 00:10:54.129
write their results to a checks folder,

s18-189
00:10:54.179 --> 00:10:56.730
and then the main session
compares the three files.

s19-190
00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.763
Start with what a sub-agent can see.

s19-191
00:10:59.813 --> 00:11:01.566
It doesn't see your conversation.

s19-192
00:11:01.616 --> 00:11:06.022
It starts with only the task description,
so the filters, the sample rules,

s19-193
00:11:06.072 --> 00:11:10.190
and the fyear convention you agreed
on in chat are invisible to it.

s19-194
00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:15.388
Put the rules on disk, and tell it to read
the metadata table and CLAUDE.md first.

s19-195
00:11:15.438 --> 00:11:18.052
Also, treat its summary as a claim.

s19-196
00:11:18.102 --> 00:11:20.879
A line like no duplicates arrives
without the evidence behind it.

s19-197
00:11:20.929 --> 00:11:24.757
Have it write the numbers to a file,
and spot-check one yourself.

s19-198
00:11:24.807 --> 00:11:25.700
As a rule of thumb,

s19-199
00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:29.769
use them for independent pieces
or output you don't need to read,

s19-200
00:11:29.819 --> 00:11:33.110
and skip them for small steps
that depend on each other.

s20-201
00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:37.021
The idea for this check
comes from Scott Cunningham,

s20-202
00:11:37.071 --> 00:11:41.898
in Claude Code 31 on his Causal
Inference Substack, March 2026.

s20-203
00:11:41.948 --> 00:11:44.738
You have Claude write the same
fixed-effects regression twice,

s20-204
00:11:44.788 --> 00:11:47.520
once in Python and once in Stata,

s20-205
00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:51.027
each from the description of the
model and not from the other script.

s20-206
00:11:51.077 --> 00:11:53.799
The coefficients should
match to six decimals.

s20-207
00:11:53.849 --> 00:11:58.507
If they don't, look for a wrong fixed
effect, a merge that duplicated rows,

s20-208
00:11:58.557 --> 00:11:59.965
or a flipped sign.

s20-209
00:12:00.015 --> 00:12:05.438
Standard errors can differ for legitimate
reasons: small-sample corrections,

s20-210
00:12:05.488 --> 00:12:08.796
singleton handling, since reghdfe
drops singletons by default,

s20-211
00:12:08.846 --> 00:12:11.335
and cluster degrees-of-freedom
adjustments.

s20-212
00:12:11.385 --> 00:12:14.930
Whatever the gap, explain it with
a named default you can point to.

s21-213
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:20.649
In the live session, the next thirty-five
minutes are the demo, on my own account,

s21-214
00:12:20.699 --> 00:12:21.688
with the tunnel already up.

s21-215
00:12:21.738 --> 00:12:23.744
I start with the feasibility prompt.

s21-216
00:12:23.794 --> 00:12:27.135
Then the funda pull through wrds-mcp,

s21-217
00:12:27.185 --> 00:12:30.176
with the iron laws and an
explicit variable list.

s21-218
00:12:30.226 --> 00:12:31.727
Then all four inspection checks,

s21-219
00:12:31.777 --> 00:12:35.360
and there's one anomaly in
there that I planted on purpose.

s21-220
00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:39.562
Then I land the data in Parquet and
DuckDB, build the metadata table,

s21-221
00:12:39.612 --> 00:12:41.734
and test it cold in a fresh session.

s21-222
00:12:41.784 --> 00:12:44.030
And I tear down the tunnel on camera.

s22-223
00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:46.469
The lab has two tracks.

s22-224
00:12:46.519 --> 00:12:51.589
The live track is for students who cleared
the WRDS checkpoint: virtual environment,

s22-225
00:12:51.639 --> 00:12:56.589
env file, tunnel up, the feasibility prompt,
a funda pull on your own account,

s22-226
00:12:56.639 --> 00:13:02.110
then inspection, Parquet, DuckDB,
the metadata table, and tunnel teardown.

s22-227
00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:07.010
The fallback track is for anyone whose Duo
isn't sorted out or whose tunnel fails.

s22-228
00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:11.205
It's the same pipeline on a public
sample built from the SEC's Financial

s22-229
00:13:11.255 --> 00:13:13.599
Statement Data Sets and shaped like funda.

s22-230
00:13:13.649 --> 00:13:16.946
Both tracks count equally,
and you can switch at any point.

s22-231
00:13:16.996 --> 00:13:20.211
The extension, on either
track, is the Stata bridge:

s22-232
00:13:20.261 --> 00:13:24.410
export an analysis-ready
.dta file, never full funda.

s23-233
00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:26.979
Then the verify checklist.

s23-234
00:13:27.029 --> 00:13:29.421
Your credential file
sits outside the project,

s23-235
00:13:29.471 --> 00:13:33.742
and a grep for your password variable
across the project comes back empty.

s23-236
00:13:33.792 --> 00:13:38.044
All four iron-law filters are in the
query, and you paste the WHERE clause.

s23-237
00:13:38.094 --> 00:13:41.542
Your four inspection checks are
written down with actual numbers.

s23-238
00:13:41.592 --> 00:13:45.799
The metadata table is queryable,
with at least eight variables documented.

s23-239
00:13:45.849 --> 00:13:50.187
There's no SELECT * against a
WRDS table or your raw extract.

s23-240
00:13:50.237 --> 00:13:56.051
And there's no WRDS-derived file outside
your WRDS data folder or project data

s23-241
00:13:56.101 --> 00:13:58.125
folder, with nothing committed to git.

s23-242
00:13:58.175 --> 00:14:00.796
On the live track, the last
box is tearing down the tunnel,

s23-243
00:14:00.846 --> 00:14:03.670
so it's the last thing
you do before you finish.

s24-244
00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:06.074
We close with a debrief.

s24-245
00:14:06.124 --> 00:14:08.320
What anomaly did your inspection catch?

s24-246
00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:10.112
I'll collect three from the room.

s24-247
00:14:10.162 --> 00:14:13.355
Which skipped iron law would
have broken whose extract?

s24-248
00:14:13.405 --> 00:14:16.793
And for the fallback track,
did the pipeline feel any different?

s24-249
00:14:16.843 --> 00:14:19.806
It shouldn't have, since
only the data source changed.

s24-250
00:14:19.856 --> 00:14:24.517
Then the exit ticket: the standard three
questions, plus one item for this module.

s24-251
00:14:24.567 --> 00:14:28.907
Write the feasibility-assessment prompt
for the data pull in your own dissertation.

s24-252
00:14:28.957 --> 00:14:31.658
Name the table, the variables,
and the date range,

s24-253
00:14:31.708 --> 00:14:35.379
and ask what might be missing
before you try anything complicated.

s24-254
00:14:35.429 --> 00:14:36.450
See you in the lab.
